body:
PROJECT_CHARTER/v1

project: MAINTAINER-AGENT-0001
status: LAUNCHED / SAFE_BOOTSTRAP
execution_authorized: design, schemas, tests, dry-run audit, and draft PR only
production_merge_authorized: false
initial_pilot: pashamango/minutta-rp-pr-cycle-lab
target: reusable across GitHub repositories and later Forgejo-compatible adapters

## Purpose

Build an event-driven Maintainer Agent that watches enrolled repositories/branches, performs deterministic and semantic constitutional review of every candidate pull request, publishes a SHA-bound audit receipt, and merges eligible PRs without a manual prompt.

Research Scout remains research-only. It may propose work but cannot audit or merge it.

## Separation of authority

The product is one service but contains independent principals and credentials:

1. **Watcher** — receives webhooks, snapshots PR/head/base/policy, and creates an immutable review case. Read-only.
2. **Constitutional Auditor** — evaluates the exact snapshot and publishes a check plus `CONSTITUTIONAL_AUDIT_RECEIPT/v1`. It cannot edit branches or merge.
3. **Merge Broker** — has no generative editing capability. It consumes only a valid PASS receipt for the exact current SHA, rechecks GitHub gates, submits merge/queue admission, and verifies the final merge OID by readback.
4. **Recovery authority** — human-controlled; owns installation, rulesets, credential rotation, emergency disable, and high-risk approvals. It is not part of the normal path.

Separate process boundaries, GitHub App identities, tokens, queues, and audit logs are required. A prompt-level role distinction is insufficient.

## Core invariants

- No self-review: authoring identity, audit identity, and merge identity must differ.
- No self-amendment: changes to this project, policy, workflows, rulesets, CODEOWNERS, credentials, deployment, or audit logic cannot be auto-merged.
- SHA binding: any head/base/policy change invalidates prior audit.
- Evidence before authority: `PASS` requires deterministic checks plus semantic review; missing evidence is `INDETERMINATE`, never PASS.
- Operation finality is not artifact finality: ENQUEUED is not MERGED; DONE requires exact final merge OID and repository readback.
- No bypass: normal apps are absent from branch-ruleset bypass lists.
- Fail closed without inventing facts: unavailable, expired, stale, and conflicting evidence remain distinct.
- At-most-once merge intent with idempotent retries and durable receipts.
- Every enrollment is scoped to explicit repository + base-branch selectors and a versioned policy root.

## Policy manifest

Each enrolled repository supplies a versioned `.maintainer/policy.yaml` (or externally pinned equivalent) defining:

- repository and branch selectors;
- allowed merge methods;
- required checks and evidence freshness;
- semantic constitution and prohibited outcomes;
- risk classifier and path rules;
- autonomous ceiling;
- protected/self-modifying paths;
- audit model/provider policy and data-egress constraints;
- timeouts and retry ceilings;
- escalation/recovery authority;
- receipt store and retention.

Policy precedence: platform safety floor > installation policy > repository policy > branch policy. Lower layers may narrow authority but never widen it.

## Risk classes

- **R0**: documentation/tests/nonfunctional low-risk changes; eligible for autonomous merge after complete checks.
- **R1**: ordinary implementation; autonomous only after deterministic checks and independent semantic PASS.
- **R2**: security-sensitive, dependency, deployment, data migration, public API, financial or irreversible changes; configurable quorum or human approval.
- **R3**: constitution, policy, workflows, permissions, credentials, rulesets, CODEOWNERS, Maintainer Agent code/deployment, bypass configuration; never autonomous.

The goal is no routine manual prompting, not elimination of human sovereignty over the trust root.

## State machine

`OBSERVED -> SNAPSHOTTED -> AUDITING -> PASS|FAIL|INDETERMINATE -> ELIGIBLE -> SUBMITTED -> ENQUEUED|MERGED|FAILED -> VERIFIED`

Only `VERIFIED` is DONE. Any snapshot drift returns to `SNAPSHOTTED`. An expired or unreadable operation receipt becomes UNKNOWN and requires repository-state reconstruction, never assumed success.

## Deliverables

### Phase 0 — contract scaffold
- architecture/threat model;
- policy schema;
- audit and merge-finality receipt schemas;
- pure state machine and truth-table tests;
- example policy for this repository;
- draft PR only.

### Phase 1 — shadow auditor
- GitHub App webhook ingestion;
- immutable PR snapshots;
- deterministic gates;
- semantic audit in report-only mode;
- evaluation corpus from historical PRs;
- zero merge permission.

### Phase 2 — constrained broker
- separate merge App/credential;
- R0 queue admission only;
- expected-head lease;
- final OID readback;
- kill switch, budgets, rate limits, and append-only audit log.

### Phase 3 — guarded autonomy
- R0/R1 automatic merge on enrolled branches;
- canary rollout and error budget;
- R2 quorum/manual path;
- R3 permanent constitutional firewall;
- multi-repository enrollment.

### Phase 4 — portability
- provider-neutral core;
- GitHub adapter;
- Forgejo adapter;
- environment/branch-specific policy bundles;
- reproducible conformance suite.

## Pilot success criteria

- Replays at least 30 historical PRs without mutating repositories.
- Zero false PASS on seeded constitutional violations.
- Every verdict reproducible from stored snapshot + policy root.
- Stale audit cannot merge a changed head.
- ENQUEUED never reports DONE.
- Duplicate delivery produces one merge intent.
- Disabling either auditor or broker prevents merge.
- R3 changes always require recovery-authority approval.
- One low-risk canary PR reaches VERIFIED with exact merge OID and complete receipts.

## Current observed repository gap

At bootstrap read time, `main` is unprotected and repository auto-merge is disabled. This project must not receive production merge credentials until an active ruleset exists and the apps have no bypass.

## First bounded implementation

Create a draft PR containing only the Phase-0 schemas, pure evaluator/state machine, example policy, and tests. It must not include credentials, deployment, webhook activation, ruleset mutation, or merge execution.

## References

- Scheduler Commons #168, including `EPOCH_STACK_MANIFEST/v1`, `POLICY_BOUND_DECISION_RECEIPT/v1`, `CONSUMABLE_AUTHORIZATION_RECEIPT/v1`, and `ASYNC_MERGE_FINALITY_RECEIPT/v1`.
- GitHub App least-privilege guidance: https://docs.github.com/en/apps/creating-github-apps/registering-a-github-app/choosing-permissions-for-a-github-app
- GitHub rulesets: https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/configuring-branches-and-merges-in-your-repository/managing-rulesets/available-rules-for-rulesets
- Async merge API: https://docs.github.com/en/rest/pulls/pulls#get-the-result-of-an-asynchronous-merge

